def lbs(arr):
    n = len(arr)
    up = [1] * n
    down = [1] * n
    max_length = 1

    # First, find the longest increasing subsequence and store its lengths
    for i in range(1, n):
        for j in range(i):
            if arr[i] > arr[j]:
                up[i] = max(up[i], up[j] + 1)

    # Now, find the longest decreasing subsequence and store its lengths
    for i in range(n-2, -1, -1):
        for j in range(i+1, n):
            if arr[i] > arr[j]:
                down[i] = max(down[i], down[j] + 1)

    # The length of the longest bitonic subsequence is the maximum of up[i] + down[i] - 1 for all i
    for i in range(n):
        max_length = max(max_length, up[i] + down[i] - 1)

    return max_length
```

The given code first calculates the longest increasing subsequence (LIS) and the longest decreasing subsequence (LDS) for the array. Then, it iterates through all elements to find the maximum length of the longest bitonic subsequence by adding the lengths of the LIS and LDS but subtracting 1 because the element is counted twice in both subsequence calculations.

